        {
            "source_entity_instance": "ENSO",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "China",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "During the non-flood season, China's precipitation is primarily governed by well-defined large-scale external forcings [77]. The atmospheric circulation is predominantly influenced by westerly systems, whose anomalies are stably teleconnected with tropical sea surface temperature anomalies, especially ENSO [78]. As a strong source of predictability, ENSO systematically modulates moisture transport and circulation over China through wave trains such as the Pacific-East Asian pattern [79].",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Middle Temperate Zone",
            "source_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "relation": "HAS_CLIMATE_ZONE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Temperate",
            "target_entity_type": "ClimateZone",
            "paragraph": "China's vast geographical area spans extensive latitudes and longitudes, with notable variations in land-sea distribution and topographic relief, resulting in diverse climate patterns. In terms of climate classification, the eastern coastal area is predominantly influenced by the monsoon climate, while the northwestern region exhibits distinct temperate continental climate characteristics (Zhao, 1995). The Tibetan Plateau is distinct from other regions due to its unique alpine climate features. Given the sensitivity of GCM performance to climatic conditions, this study subdivides the land areas of China into climate zones and performs spatial and temporal skill assessments of GCMs for each zone. Specifically, mainland China is subdivided into nine major climate zones: the Sub-frigid Zone (I), the Middle Temperate Zone (II), the Warm Temperate Zone (III), the Northern Subtropical Zone (IV), the Central Subtropical Zone (V), the Southern Subtropical Zone (VI), the Marginal Tropical Zone (VII), the Middle Tropical Zone (VIII), and the Tibetan Plateau Region (H).",
